---
// The OpenAI Decisions API as benchmarked: its accuracy, confidence, speed and price, each from the
// scored rows, with OpenAI's own claims (lib/content.js DECISIONS_API) kept apart from our measurements.
import { data, urls, OWA, CWA, TASK_INFO, taskOrder, pct, pts, fmt, int, overall, axisRow, pairRow, latency, costOf, listOf } from "../lib/site.js";
import { DECISIONS_API } from "../lib/content.js";
const API = "openai-decisions-gpt-6-luna";
const CHAT = "openai-gpt-6-luna-effort-none";
const S = DECISIONS_API.sources;
const o = (id, s) => overall(id, s);
const share = (id, s, gold, said) => { const row = o(id, s) && o(id, s).confusion[gold]; if (!row) return null; const n = Object.values(row).reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0); return n ? row[said] / n : null; };
const conf = (s) => data.probe && data.probe.tasks[s] && data.probe.tasks[s].decisions_api_confidence;
const lat = latency(API, OWA);
const cost = costOf(API);
const have = !!(o(API, OWA) && o(API, CWA));
---
{have && (
<section class="wrap section sec-prose" id="decisions-api" aria-labelledby="decisions-api-h">
  <h2 class="display" id="decisions-api-h">The OpenAI Decisions API, as we tested it</h2>
  <p class="lede">OpenAI's Decisions API is generally available (<a href={S.announcement.url}>{S.announcement.label}</a>). A request goes to <code>POST /v1/decisions</code> with the model <code>gpt-6-luna</code>, and each question is a predicate, a choice or a score. A choice answer returns the chosen option, a probability for every option and a separate confidence (<a href={S.guide.url}>{S.guide.label}</a>). We sent one choice question per problem, with the instructions and option descriptions every other model received.</p>
  <h3>Accuracy</h3>
  <p>It answered {pct(o(API, OWA).accuracy)}% of open-world problems and {pct(o(API, CWA).accuracy)}% of closed-world ones correctly. Jev answered {pct(o("jev", OWA).accuracy)}% and {pct(o("jev", CWA).accuracy)}%; {taskOrder.map((s) => { const r = pairRow(s, "jev", API, "overall", "all"); return r ? `${pts(r.diff)} points on the ${TASK_INFO[s].short.toLowerCase()} task (95% interval ${pts(r.ci_low)} to ${pts(r.ci_high)}) on the same items` : null; }).filter(Boolean).join(", and ")}. GPT-6 Luna through the chat endpoint with reasoning off, the same model name, scored {pct(o(CHAT, OWA).accuracy)}% and {pct(o(CHAT, CWA).accuracy)}%, so the Decisions API scored {fmt(100 * (o(CHAT, OWA).accuracy - o(API, OWA).accuracy), 1)} and {fmt(100 * (o(CHAT, CWA).accuracy - o(API, CWA).accuracy), 1)} points lower on the same problems. We do not know why. We did not test other wordings of the instructions.</p>
  {axisRow(API, OWA, "depth", "0") && axisRow(API, OWA, "depth", "5") && <p>By depth on the open-world task: {pct(axisRow(API, OWA, "depth", "0").accuracy, 0)}% when the answer is stated outright (depth 0), falling to {pct(axisRow(API, OWA, "depth", "5").accuracy, 0)}% at five chained inferences. At depth 0 the request and the scoring work as intended; the loss is in the deeper problems.</p>}
  <p>On the open-world task it answered unknown for {pct(share(API, OWA, "true", "unknown"), 0)}% of problems whose correct answer is true and {pct(share(API, OWA, "false", "unknown"), 0)}% of those whose answer is false; Jev did so for {pct(share("jev", OWA, "true", "unknown"), 0)}% and {pct(share("jev", OWA, "false", "unknown"), 0)}%. On the closed-world task it answered true for {pct(share(API, CWA, "false", "true"), 0)}% of problems whose answer is false ({pct(share(CHAT, CWA, "false", "true"), 0)}% for GPT-6 Luna through chat, {pct(share("jev", CWA, "false", "true"), 0)}% for Jev).</p>
  <h3>Confidence</h3>
  {conf(OWA) && conf(CWA) && <p>The response carries a confidence beside its probabilities. We checked whether it separates right answers from wrong ones (AUROC, where 0.5 is no better than chance and 1 is perfect): {fmt(conf(OWA).auroc, 2)} on the open-world task and {fmt(conf(CWA).auroc, 2)} on the closed-world task. Probabilities are rounded to two decimals, so the confidence took only {int(conf(OWA).distinct_values)} distinct values on the open-world task and {int(conf(CWA).distinct_values)} on the closed-world one.</p>}
  <h3>Speed and price</h3>
  <p>OpenAI says the API "returns typed answers about 10x faster than the Responses API" (<a href={S.guide.url}>{S.guide.label}</a>); that is OpenAI's claim, and we did not compare against the Responses API.{lat ? <Fragment> What we measured: a median of {int(Math.round(lat.p50))} ms per request and a 90th percentile of {int(Math.round(lat.p90))} ms over the network, in the scored run, with {lat.concurrency} requests at a time. That includes network time and is not like for like with models that ran on a laptop.</Fragment> : null}</p>
  {cost && <p>At OpenAI's list price of $0.10 per million input tokens (output tokens are not charged), the 3,600 problems cost ${fmt(cost.total.usd, 2)}, or ${fmt(cost.total.usd_per_million_correct, 0)} per million correct decisions. <a href={urls.cost()}>Cost per decision for every hosted model</a>.</p>}
  <p class="more-link"><a href={urls.preview()}>The Decisions API benchmark in full</a>, with a verbatim request and response.</p>
</section>
)}
